    "text": "Protagonist Design\n\nIn this process of translating personal trauma into game mechanics, the protagonist is more than just a playable avatar; she is a projection of my own uncompromising inner self.\n\nTechnical Implementation\n\nLacking a formal background in 3D art and animation, I utilized Hyper3D to generate the character model and manually rigged and animated it using Blender. As a result, the character's animations lack the seamless fluidity of commercial titles, carrying instead an undeniable sense of stiffness.\n\nDesign Metaphor\n\nInitially, I was frustrated by this lack of fluid motion. However, I quickly realized that this stilted, slightly rigid physical feedback accurately captures the harsh reality of sports rehabilitation. The slight clunkiness and hesitation in the protagonist's dribbling and dodging perfectly mirror the steep decline in physical capability following a severe injury. The once-effortless muscle memory is gone, and the connection between nerve and limb is broken. Every simple crossover now requires deliberate mental exertion to overcome subconscious physical fear. In this way, a technical \"imperfection\" organically elevated the absolute authenticity of the emotional narrative.\n\nThe Faceless Player\n\nThe model generated by Hyper3D lacks intricate facial details, a trait that coincidentally aligns with the psychological state of recovery. Through the agonizing, monotonous repetition of daily physical therapy, outward individuality is gradually stripped away. What drives the protagonist forward—dribbling past obstacle after obstacle—is not the glamour of the spotlight, but a pure, desperate will to \"heal.\" Within the game, she takes the place of my injured self, limping yet relentless, carrying forward an unfinished basketball dream."
