    "text": "1 Level Progression & Design Intention\n\nThe obstacle design and level progression are intentionally structured to mirror the psychological and physical timeline of sports rehabilitation. The overarching design intention is clear: the player must first conquer her internal fears and physical limitations (Phase I: Overcoming the Self) before she is resilient enough to face external malice and intense physical confrontation (Phase II: Confronting the Outside World).\n\n2 The Metaphor of the Loop (Level 1 & Level 2)\n\nStructurally, both Phase I and Phase II share an identical progression loop: enduring randomized obstacles until a 500-meter milestone is reached. This repetitive, highly monotonous structure is not a lack of design variation, but a deliberate metaphor for the reality of rehabilitation training. Recovery is rarely a fast-paced adventure; it is a tedious, cyclical, and often frustrating grind. By forcing the player to repeat the core loop until the exact distance is cleared, the gameplay mechanically replicates the immense patience and endurance required to slowly rebuild a shattered body.\n\nLevel progression diagram\n\nLevel flow: Levels 1 and 2 start with Tutorial Mode, then Training & Action and Training Action. Distance under 500 m loops to Training & Action; at 500 m or more, trigger a scene transition and load the next stage. Level 3 enters the final shot, reads the defender and finds an opening, asks for Space within 24 seconds, triggers the final ending, then ends the game.\n\nObstacle Metaphors\n\nCore Penalty Rule\n\nThroughout the game, failing to execute a dodge or missing a Quick Time Event (QTE) shares a unified consequence: Turnover & Retrieval. The player loses possession of the ball, forcing her to break momentum and physically run back to retrieve it. This shared penalty constantly reinforces the frustration of rehab—where hard-earned progress can be instantly undone by a single mistake."
